The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```c
qi_cache = kmem_cache_create("caamqicache", CAAM_QI_MEMCACHE_SIZE, 0,
                             SLAB_CACHE_DMA, NULL);
if (!qi_cache) {
    dev_err(qidev, "Can't allocate CAAM cache\n");
    free_rsp_fqs();
    platform_device_unregister(qi_pdev);
    return err;
}
```
Reason:
The bug occurs because when `kmem_cache_create()` fails, the code incorrectly returns `err` (which is uninitialized and defaults to 0) instead of the appropriate error code `-ENOMEM`. This misrepresentation can lead to the system assuming that the initialization was successful when it was not.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
-    return err;
+    return -ENOMEM;
```
Reason:
The fix changes the return value from `err` to `-ENOMEM` when `kmem_cache_create()` fails. This correctly indicates that there was an error due to insufficient memory, allowing the calling functions to handle the error appropriately and preventing further issues related to the incorrect assumption of successful initialization.

